> Docs\LEVEL1.md:1:# Level 1
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:2:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:3:Written October 21, 2026, after Bryan's second headset test. Level 1 is the first playable: one PC, 
one headset, one printed page, about three minutes a run. It is what we build and test next. The slice and the stages 
stay in the repo unchanged; what they add beyond Level 1 (the order lag, permits and codes, inspections, rain, field 
mistakes, money, the hub model) comes back later, one level at a time.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:4:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:5:Decisions K29 to K36 in `Docs/DECISIONS.md`. Scenario `Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json` (WO-L1). 
Rules page `Paper/level1_rules.md`. Headset WO-L2.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:6:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:7:## The job
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:8:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:9:- 3,000 ft of water main in Texas. Stakes every 100 ft; 5+20 is 520 ft from the start.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:10:- Three crews with one Standard machine each, in this order along the trench: Excavation (Ray) 220 
ft a day, Pipe (Marcus) 180, Backfill (Dana) 250. A crew never passes the crew in front of it.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:11:- The PM's plan: excavation starts Day 1, pipe Day 2, backfill Day 4. The PM promised Day 18.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:12:- One thing goes wrong. When pipe reaches 5+20 (about Day 4) the trench wall there falls in, and 
pipe laying stops for 2 days while it is cleared. Then pipe goes on by itself.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:13:- If a crew catches the crew in front, the two foremen argue and both crews stop until Plan holds 
the crew behind. This happens once per pair; after that a crew that catches up just waits, and the foremen grumble.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:14:- A crew that has not started is not on site yet: holding it delays its start and costs nothing. 
Once a crew has started, every day it stands still is an idle crew-day.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:15:- The clock runs 4 s per day, and 15 s per day while the wall is down or the foremen are arguing.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:16:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:17:## The seats
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:18:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:19:| | Site (headset) | Plan (PC) | Rules (paper) |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:20:| --- | --- | --- | --- |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:21:| Sees | The site live: the crews, the trench, the fallen wall, the water. Hears the foremen | The 
chart: the PM's planned lines and what the crews reported up to yesterday | One page: what Site might describe, what 
it is, how long it lasts, the crew speeds |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:22:| Does | Picks a crew and warps to it, looks, comes back, talks | Start, and Hold or Go for each 
crew | Says what it is and how long it lasts; works out when one crew will catch another |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:23:| Cannot | Change anything | See the site, or where the crews are today | See the site or the 
chart |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:24:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:25:Nothing tells the team whether a choice was right. The PM's voice pushes to keep every crew moving.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:26:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:27:## Starting a game
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:28:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:29:1. On the PC, double-click `Play LOB Over.cmd` in the LOB-OVER folder. A window opens (leave it 
open) and the Plan page opens in the browser. The first time, Windows asks whether Node may use the network: allow it 
on private networks.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:30:2. Put on the headset and open LOB, Over. It finds the PC by itself: over the USB cable if one is 
plugged in, otherwise over Wi-Fi with the PC and the headset on the same network. Campus Wi-Fi often stops devices 
from seeing each other; then use the cable or a phone hotspot. The Plan page says "Headset connected".
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:31:3. Give the Rules player the printed page (the Plan page links it).
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:32:4. Plan presses Start.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:33:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:34:## In the headset
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:35:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:36:Revised October 22, after the fourth build (the tunnel removed): one site, everything in front, 
real scale, words you can read, days you can see. Decisions K45 to K48; work order WO-L8.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:37:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:38:- You start in your room, in full passthrough, and can see and talk to the others. Nothing is 
drawn but a panel within arm's reach: the day, the connection, and one button, Go to the site.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:39:- Touch it. The site materializes around you where you stand: a wave runs outward from your feet; 
just behind its front everything is glowing wire and blueprint lines over the room, then the surfaces fill in, until 
the room is gone and the sky closes over you. You do not move; the site is built around you.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:40:- You stand on the road. The trench runs across in front of you, 6 m away. The three crews work in 
front of you at real size: the pipe crew in the middle, the excavator to the right, the backfill loader to the left, 
nearer or farther as the real gaps between them change. Machines crawl as they work; you never move. The stake by each 
crew shows its real station. The rest of the site runs off to the horizon on both sides.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:41:- Look at a crew for two seconds and you have walked over to it: its foreman greets you or says 
what is going on, and the host treats you as standing there. Whatever anyone says shows in a speech bubble over their 
head while it plays. A Log button on your wrist opens a panel in front of you with the last twenty lines, the day and 
the clock state; grab it and leave it anywhere.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:42:- Each day the sun crosses the sky; at the day boundary the light dims to dusk and the new day 
opens with its number in front of you. When the wall falls you see it fall in front of you; when the foremen argue, 
they meet in front of you.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:43:- Back on your left wrist dissolves the site into your room.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:44:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:45:## On the PC
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:46:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:47:Revised later on October 21: the first page worked but did not feel like a game (WO-L3).
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:48:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:49:The Plan seat is the site office. The Line of Balance is a drafting board, inked day by day as the 
reports come in, with the PM's planned lines dashed under it and a ruler sliding down the days. Beside it is the radio 
console: one card per crew with its foreman, machine and speed, and a two-position switch, Go or Hold, that clunks and 
sends a radio chirp. The PM has a face (LOB-Game's portraits) and a speech bubble when a line plays. A calendar tile 
flips each day with a tick; when the field slows the clock pill says so and the radio hisses, and nothing on the page 
says why. A daily report line types itself at the bottom. At the end the result is stamped on the board: the finish 
against the promise and the idle crew-days per crew, with the actual lines over the plan; Run again keeps the previous 
run faint underneath. Nothing on the page shows the live site, money, or a verdict.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:50:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:51:## How a run goes
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:52:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:53:Push, the PM's plan:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:54:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:55:1. Plan presses Start. Site warps to Marcus and watches pipe go in; at the excavator, Ray says the 
ground is good and they will make their footage.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:56:2. About Day 4 the wall at 5+20 falls in. Site sees it and hears Marcus get his crew out. Site 
describes it. Rules finds it on the page: a collapse, pipe stops for 2 days. Plan's chart will not show anything until 
tomorrow's report.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:57:3. Dana's backfill started Day 4 right behind pipe. Within a day it reaches Marcus's crew and the 
two foremen argue: 250 ft a day against 180, and whoever wrote the start dates. Both crews stop. Site hears it and 
says so. Plan holds backfill, which ends the argument.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:58:4. When should Plan press Go for backfill? Rules works it out from the page: pipe needs about 17 
days for 3,000 ft plus the 2 days lost, backfill needs 12, so backfill can start well after pipe and still finish 
right behind it. Too early and Dana waits behind Marcus again; too late and the job finishes later.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:59:5. The result: the finish day against Day 18, and the idle crew-days.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:60:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:61:Pull, run again on the same world: the team holds backfill before Day 4 and presses Go when pipe 
is far enough ahead. The wall still falls, but Dana is far behind, so there is no argument. Starting backfill early 
barely changes the finish, because pipe sets the pace; mostly it buys idle crew-days and a fight. That is Level 1's 
lesson.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:62:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:63:Measured runs (filled in by WO-L1 from `Monitor/test/scripts/level1_push.json` and 
`level1_pull.json`):
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:64:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:65:| Run | Finish (t, display day) | Idle crew-days (exc, pipe, back) | Argument |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:66:| --- | --- | --- | --- |
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:67:| Push: hold backfill at the argument, Go two days later | 19.67, Day 20 | 0.004, 2.003, 4.670 | 
Yes |
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:70:## Voices
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:71:
> Docs\LEVEL1.md:72:Foremen are heard only in the headset; the PM only on the Plan page. Each line plays once. An 
exchange plays turn by turn, each from its speaker's crew, 0.3 s apart, and exchanges never overlap. The texts follow 
`Docs/VOICE.md`: claims, not conclusions.
