spentUsd":262900,"idleCrewDays":9.5,"peakOpenTrenchFt":1310,"finesUsd":0,"deviationsCaught":1,"deviationsMissed":0,"eve
nts":[...],"changeLog":[...],"lines":{"planned":[...],"actual":[...]},"previousRun":null|{...}}
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:71:```
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:72:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:73:## Per-seat views (what `state.view` contains)
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:74:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:75:**Plan** (`seat: "plan"`): `plannedLines` (per crew: start day, segments with slope from the 
machine rate, holds), `responses[]` (the `pay` options available right now: `key`, `label`, `cost`, `eventTitle`; 
empty when no event is active or forecast), `reportedLines` (actual feet per crew as of the last daily report; never 
today's), `budget` (`budgetUsd`, `spentUsd`, `committedUsd`, `deadlineDay`, `promisedDay`), `inbox[]` (dated items 
from the scenario plus daily reports), `radioLog[]` (orders with status), `catalog` (machines, protection, prices, 
lead days incl. the city delay), `permits` (per zone: `entered: true|false`, `number` if entered; never whether 
entering was wise), `inspections` (per zone: cleared or not), `flags[]` (station, day; no text unless Site typed a 
note), `draft` (ops not yet committed), `uncertainty` (ranges per crew from SR11-style bands; narrows when a report 
lands), `pmLines[]` (text of PM voice lines played so far).
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:76:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:77:**Site** (`seat: "site"`): `truck` (`stationFt`, `moving`, `dismounted`), `crews[]` (`id`, 
`stationFt` live, `working|idle|held|stopped|arguing|demobilized`, `machinePlate` ("Standard"), `moodLevel` 0-3), 
`trench[]` per 100 ft station (`state`: `undug|open|piped|backfilled`, `water: none|seep|standing`, `wall: 
sound|slumped`, `protection: none|box|shoring|slope`, `pipeDamage: none|cracked`), `boards[]` per zone 
(`permitNumber|null`, `inspectionNumber|null`), `sky` (`clear|towersSouth|rain`), `gate` (`trucksWaiting`), 
`activeEvent` (facts only, if Site is within the zone or has inspected it), `orders[]` landed at Site's current 
station awaiting confirmation. No plan, no money, no forecast percentages.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:78:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:79:**Facilitator** (`seat: "facilitator"`): everything, including hidden variants, pending events 
and deviations, the two other views side by side. Never shown to players; used by Bryan during table tests and by 
Playwright during verification.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:80:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:81:**Rules** (`seat: "rules"`): exactly `{stage, displayDay, speed}` in `state.view`. No positions, 
plan, money, daily reports, field events or result overlay. On welcome and reset/load it also receives `kit`: 
`{t:"kit", v:1, stage, title, job:{sign:{cityCode,pipe,contractDays},lengthFt,zones}, permits:{cards}, 
inspections:{cards,checklist}, manualPages}`. These are authored job sign, card and ledger facts, not live simulation 
state. The page loads the filled manual from `manual/<stage>.html`; signatures, checkboxes and ledger notes remain in 
that page and reset with the run. Rules can send `ready`; simulation commands are unavailable to it.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:82:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:83:## Guided playthrough (WO-S7)
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:84:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:85:The Node host accepts `--guide Data/Guides/slice_guided.json`. The browser host offers Guided 
and a guide picker. Enabling Guided resets the run and turns Autoplan off; it can be enabled again explicitly. The 
selected guide must match the selected scenario. Without a guide, the existing clock and commands are unchanged.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:86:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:87:On Start, and at each subsequent trigger, the host pauses and sends each player only their own 
prompt:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:88:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:89:```json
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:93:`index` is zero-based. Only the facilitator's message adds `narration`, 
`prompts:{site,plan,rules}`, and `beats:[{id,title,status}]`, where status is `upcoming`, `current`, `done` or 
`skipped`. No guide source or other seat's prompt is sent to a player. Between beats, `beat` and `rulesSection` are 
null and `prompt` is empty; player banners hide. The facilitator keeps the beat list. Joining a running guide receives 
its current message.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:94:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:95:Beats trigger in order on start, authored event id, conflict pair (`back-pipe` corresponds to 
event id `conflict_back_pipe`), simulation time, backfill within 10 ft of a zone end (including idle at the hold 
point), or result. The host observes the completed simulation tick: conflict events carry the beginning-of-tick time 
and pause at the end of that 0.01-day tick. If a later trigger fires before an earlier trigger, the earlier beat is 
skipped. Triggers reached during a paused trip are retained until the current beat ends.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:96:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:97:`cmd ready` marks the sending seat ready, broadcasts the updated ready map and acknowledges 
once. Once every connected primary player seat is ready, the host waits the guide's `resumeDelaySeconds` of real time, 
then resumes. Read-only mirrors and the facilitator do not count; no connected players means no automatic resume. A 
newly connected unready seat cancels a pending countdown. Readiness resets for every beat. `ctrl continue` ends the 
current beat immediately; any already-triggered next beat pauses immediately. Start/Resume cannot bypass a current 
guide beat. Reset/load cancel pending countdowns. With no active beat, Ready/Continue return `nack: "no guide beat"`.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:98:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:99:Commands remain available during guide pauses. A valid Site `moveTo` while paused advances only 
the simulation travel duration (`|Δft| / 1500 / 24` days), including the fractional final tick, then pauses again. 
Other commands do not advance the clock. Guided Site receives only `{t:"result",v:1,stage}` at the result; Plan and 
the facilitator receive the full overlay. Rules receives the result beat, with its own prompt.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:100:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:101:## Daily report timing
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:102:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:103:At each day boundary the host emits `report` for the day that just ended and advances 
`reportedLines` for Plan. Site's view is live at all times. This is the one authored asymmetry everything else depends 
on; do not "fix" it.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:104:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:105:## Phase 1 note
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:106:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:107:The Unity host implements the same messages; the Quest client replaces `site.html`. The C# port 
is accepted only when the headless Stage 1 transcript (`state` snapshots at each day boundary plus every `event`, 
`report`, `result`) matches the Node host's transcript for the same scenario and seed within the golden tolerances.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:108:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:109:## Level 1 additions (WO-L1)
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:110:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:111:`rules.planControlsClock: true` allows the primary Plan connection to send `ctrl start`
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:112:and `ctrl reset`. Other controls remain facilitator-only; mirrors remain read-only.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:113:The host starts paused. The opening PM line is delivered on Start, once per run.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:114:During an argument, Plan may only commit one `hold` for the trailing crew; other
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:115:orders return `The field won't take that order right now` without naming the problem.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:117:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:118:For this mode, Plan's view contains `plannedLines`, `plannedPolylines` (the original
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:119:PM plan), `reportedLines` (through yesterday), `held` (booleans keyed by crew),
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:120:`promisedDay`, `deadlineDay`, and authored `crews` with `id`, `name`, `foreman`,
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:121:`startDay`, `rate` (ft/day). `project` and `plan.locked` retain their meanings.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:122:`siteClient` is the primary Site connection's hello `client`: `quest`, `web`, or
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:123:`null` when disconnected. `previousRun` contains the prior run's actual polylines,
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:124:or null. These additions carry no live field positions to Plan. Reports omit money.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:125:Site crews add `onSite`: false before their start or while an unstarted crew is held.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:126:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:127:At the result Plan receives only `t`, `v`, `stage`, `finishDay`, `promisedDay`,
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:128:`idleDays` (per crew), `lines` (`planned`, `actual`), and `previousRun` with the same
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:129:public result fields. Site receives only `{t:"result",v:1,stage}`. The facilitator
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:130:retains the full result; money remains internal to the simulation and facilitator.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:131:Reset retains the previous completed run for the faint chart overlay.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:132:
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:133:New voice messages retain `lineIds` and add `turns`, in speaking order:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:134:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:135:```json
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:136:{"t":"voice","v":1,"day":3.89,"lineIds":["marcus_collapse"],"seats":["site"],"turns":[{"speaker"
:"foreman_pipe","text":"Wall's moving! Out of the hole, everybody out, 
now!","clip":"marcus_collapse_1.wav","stationFt":520.2}]}
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:137:```
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:138:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:139:The host also includes the authored line objects in `lines`, including `turns` for
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:140:exchanges. Play each `turns[].clip` from `/Voice/<stage>/`, at its speaker's crew
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:141:position (`stationFt` is omitted for PM/radio). Do not overlap exchanges; leave
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:142:0.3 real seconds between turns. No captions during play. PM reaches Plan only;
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:143:foremen reach Site only. Arrivals can repeat on a new display day, at most once
> Docs\PROTOCOL.md:144:per crew per day; repeat grumbles at most once per pair per day. Other lines play
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:145:once per run. Listen near a crew replays its last exchange.
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:146:
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:147:`/play.html` is the Level 1 PC page. Its Rules link serves `/level1/rules.pdf`;
  Docs\PROTOCOL.md:148:`/level1/rules.html` is also available. Existing stage pages are retained.
# Voices

Written October 21, 2026. Who speaks, what a voice may say, and the line sheet for the slice. Applies to every scenario; WO-S10 implements it.

## The problem to avoid

The headset player hears voices the other two cannot. If a voice says what happened and what to do, the headset player repeats it, the other two act on it, and nobody works out what is different from the plan or what the problem actually is. The game becomes relay without reasoning. A voice must hand the team something to work out together, never the answer.

## Principles

1. **Claims, not conclusions.** A voice says what the speaker sees, feels or wants. It never names the situation code, the cause in manual terms, the days lost, the response options, or what to do. LOB-Game's PM event lines already work this way ("A trench wall came down. The pipe crew has stopped.").
2. **Every claim is checkable by another seat.** A foreman's number is a rate, a day or a station that Plan can find on the LOB or Rules can run through the front-page rule. A claim nobody can check is noise.
3. **Speakers are partial.** Each foreman argues for his crew and blames the other crew or the schedule. Both are partly right; the team reconciles them with the LOB and the manual.
4. **Problems hide in normal lines.** Deviations are never announced. The excavation foreman whose order never arrived sounds cheerful about a good digging day. Only a Site player who knows what the office ordered hears the problem, and only Plan can tell Site what was ordered.
5. **Each voice reaches one seat.** Foremen are heard in the headset only (or on `site.html`). The PM and the radio play on the Plan laptop; the PM also appears as the hub standee for the morning brief. Rules hears nothing; Rules has paper. Whatever matters crosses the table in someone's own words.
6. **Once only.** A line plays once. Listen near a crew replays that crew's last line. No transcript is shown during play; the end-of-stage change log lists who said what and when.
7. **No verdict voices.** No line responds to whether a decision was good. The PM pushes against holds, demobilizations and purchases whatever the outcome; the end line states facts.

## Cast

| Speaker id | Name | Character | Heard on | Placeholder voice |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `foreman_exc` | Ray | Excavation foreman, steady, proud of his footage | headset, `site.html` | American male |
| `foreman_pipe` | Marcus | Pipe foreman, careful, quick to anger about safety | headset, `site.html` | a second American male, clearly different from Ray |
| `foreman_back` | Dana | Backfill foreman, impatient, paid by the foot | headset, `site.html` | American female |
| `pm` | the PM | Pushes the schedule, hates idle crews | Plan laptop; hub standee for the morning brief | older American male |
| `radio` | the dispatcher | Relays orders, flat | Plan laptop | neutral American female, radio treatment |

Names are spoken in the lines so players can say "Dana says...". Kokoro voice ids live in `Tools/voice/voices.json`.

## Triggers

Existing: `stageStart`, `prepRefused`, `holdCommitted`, `demobCommitted`, `orderLanded`, `orderNotDone`, `conflict`, `heldDay1` to `heldDay3`, `stageEnd`.

New in WO-S10:
- `event` with `eventType` (P1, A2, ...): a foreman's reaction where the event happens.
- `arrive` with `crew` and `status`: Site stands within 30 m of that crew; at most once per crew per day. For `working` this is the everyday chatter that can hide a deviation.
- `sky` with `state` (`towersSouth`): a foreman remarks on the weather when it turns.
